= Introduction

== A queue on a July afternoon

At 14:00 a bus shelter holds a short queue and one strip of shade. Catalogue ratings promise several kelvin of cooling at the nozzle; the passenger at the far end of the bench feels little of it. Entrainment, buoyant dive, and cross-ventilation eat the jet before it becomes comfort. The procurement question becomes: which unit delivers felt relief that people can use, under a safety line and a budget?

== Why the problem is hard

Open and semi-open spaces break sealed-room efficiency. Fans move air without lowering dry-bulb; evaporative pads lower dry-bulb while adding moisture and water cost; larger supply jets can *detrain* more cold air per metre. Rankings that ignore delivery, perception, coverage, safety, or lifecycle cost will recommend machines that cool the pavement or the spreadsheet rather than the queue.

== Our work

We rebuild the delivery-efficiency argument as a testable three-layer chain (M1 → M2 → M3), replace an inconsistent jet-fit delivery fraction with an elementary fluids layer, keep CEI as the decision scalar, and add safety and budget as hard gates. An E6-style ablation asks how much the 63 % humidity threshold moves when delivery physics changes—the question judges ask first.
